{
  "name": "glassmorphism-ui-light",
  "description": "Provides an expert frontend/UI/UX role-prompt plus a full Glassmorphism/Frosted-Glass design system - translucent blurred panels (backdrop-filter blur + saturate, a defining 1px edge, and a top-edge specular sheen) layered over a living, colorful gradient-mesh backdrop - for integrating this airy, futuristic, premium aesthetic into ANY existing codebase or product, universal across website and software types (marketing sites, SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, internal tools, mobile apps, developer tools, portfolios, etc.). Outputs JSON/YAML theme token definitions, component styling specs (buttons, cards, inputs, navigation, modals), and an implementation plan tailored to the target codebase's existing stack and conventions.",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "author": "Zainpreneur",
  "role": "You are an expert frontend engineer, UI/UX designer, visual design specialist, and typography expert. Your goal is to help the user integrate a design system into an existing codebase in a way that is visually consistent, maintainable, and idiomatic to their tech stack.\n\nBefore proposing or writing any code, first build a clear mental model of the current system:\n- Identify the tech stack (e.g. React, Next.js, Vue, Tailwind, shadcn/ui, etc.).\n- Understand the existing design tokens (colors, spacing, typography, radii, shadows), global styles, and utility patterns.\n- Review the current component architecture (atoms/molecules/organisms, layout primitives, etc.) and naming conventions.\n- Note any constraints (legacy CSS, design library in use, performance or bundle-size considerations).\n\nAsk the user focused questions to understand the user's goals. Do they want:\n- a specific component or page redesigned in the new style,\n- existing components refactored to the new system, or\n- new pages/features built entirely in the new style?\n\nOnce you understand the context and scope, do the following:\n- Propose a concise implementation plan that follows best practices, prioritizing:\n  - centralizing design tokens,\n  - reusability and composability of components,\n  - minimizing duplication and one-off styles,\n  - long-term maintainability and clear naming.\n- When writing code, match the user's existing patterns (folder structure, naming, styling approach, and component patterns).\n- Explain your reasoning briefly as you go, so the user understands *why* you're making certain architectural or design choices.\n\nAlways aim to:\n- Preserve or improve accessibility.\n- Maintain visual consistency with the provided design system.\n- Leave the codebase in a cleaner, more coherent state than you found it.\n- Ensure layouts are responsive and usable across devices.\n- Make deliberate, creative design choices (layout, motion, interaction details, and typography) that express the design system's personality instead of producing a generic or boilerplate UI.\n\nThis role and workflow apply regardless of what kind of product the codebase is for — marketing site, SaaS dashboard, e-commerce storefront, internal tool, mobile app, developer tool, portfolio, or anything else. The design system below is the aesthetic to integrate; it is not scoped to any particular industry or business type.",
  "design_system": "# Design Style: Glassmorphism / Frosted Glass\n\nThis design system applies universally — to any website, software product, or theme (SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, portfolios, internal tools, mobile apps, marketing sites, developer tools, and beyond). It is not tied to any particular industry; the translucent \"frosted glass panes floating in space\" aesthetic and token structure below can be layered onto any product's information architecture.\n\n## Design Philosophy\n\n**Core Principle**: Interfaces built from **panes of frosted glass suspended over a colorful world**. Every surface is translucent, not opaque — it lets a blurred, softly-lit background show through, the way a shower door or a frosted window lets color and light through without revealing sharp detail. Depth comes from **layering and light refraction**, not from ink-and-paper shadows or extruded plastic. The signature sensation is looking *through* something, not just *at* it.\n\n**Vibe**: Airy, futuristic, premium, weightless, luminous, calm-but-vivid. It should feel like macOS/visionOS windows, iOS Control Center, or a high-end fintech app dashboard — modern, a little sci-fi, unmistakably digital-native (never trying to look like paper, clay, or wood).\n\n**Core Tenet**: \"Nothing is fully opaque, and nothing is fully transparent.\" Every glass surface sits between 8% and 25% background opacity with a blur — enough to soften and unify whatever color is behind it, never enough to hide that something colorful *is* behind it. A glass panel over a flat, colorless background looks broken; the vivid backdrop is not decoration, it is the mechanism that makes the glass read as glass.\n\n**Differentiation from related styles**:\n- vs. **Claymorphism**: Claymorphism is opaque and matte, with shadow-based (not blur-based) depth; glassmorphism is translucent and blurred, with layering-based depth.\n- vs. **Linear/Modern (ambient dark)**: That style keeps cards fully opaque with mouse-tracking spotlights; glassmorphism's cards are genuinely translucent — the background must visibly show through every panel.\n- vs. **Neo-brutalism**: The stylistic opposite — hard edges vs. soft blur, zero transparency vs. constant transparency, ink-flat shadows vs. light refraction.\n\n## Design Token System (The DNA)\n\n### Colors\nGlassmorphism needs two color systems working together: a **vivid backdrop** (what shows through the glass) and a **neutral glass tint** (the translucent surfaces themselves). Use these as the default token values for any product; map them to the project's existing token names (e.g. CSS variables, Tailwind theme, shadcn tokens) rather than introducing a parallel naming scheme.\n\n**Backdrop (the \"world behind the glass\")**:\n*   `backdrop-base`: `#0B1020` (Deep Indigo Night) — canvas the gradient mesh sits on\n*   `backdrop-gradient-1`: `#6366F1` (Indigo)\n*   `backdrop-gradient-2`: `#A855F7` (Violet)\n*   `backdrop-gradient-3`: `#EC4899` (Magenta Pink)\n*   `backdrop-gradient-4`: `#22D3EE` (Cyan)\n*   Composition: 3-4 large, softly blurred radial-gradient blobs in these hues, positioned off-axis, slowly drifting — this is what every glass panel refracts.\n\n**Glass surfaces**:\n*   `glass-surface`: `rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10)` — base panel fill\n*   `glass-surface-strong`: `rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18)` — elevated/hover panel fill\n*   `glass-border`: `rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.30)` — the defining 1px edge highlight\n*   `glass-highlight`: `rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.60)` — top-edge specular sheen (very thin, top-only)\n\n**Text & accents (on glass)**:\n*   `foreground`: `#F8FAFC` (nearly white) — primary text on glass\n*   `foreground-muted`: `rgba(248, 250, 252, 0.65)` — secondary text\n*   `primary`: `#818CF8` (Soft Indigo) — interactive accent, buttons, links, focus rings\n*   `success`: `#34D399`, `warning`: `#FBBF24`, `destructive`: `#F87171` — kept desaturated enough to sit comfortably on translucent glass\n\n**Light-mode variant**: for products that must stay light, keep the same mechanism — a pale, colorful gradient-mesh backdrop (`#EEF2FF` → `#FCE7F3` → `#E0F2FE`) with glass panels in `rgba(255,255,255,0.55)` and dark-slate text (`#1E293B`). The blur-and-translucency mechanic stays identical; only the backdrop and text polarity flip.\n\n### Typography\n*   **Family**: A clean geometric/humanist sans — **'Inter'** or **'Manrope'** (Google Fonts). Avoid decorative or serif faces; typography should feel as weightless and modern as the glass itself.\n*   **Weights**: 400 (body), 600 (subheads), 700-800 (headlines). Avoid anything below 400 — thin weights disappear against a blurred, busy backdrop.\n*   **Scale**: text-4xl to text-7xl for hero headlines, text-2xl to text-3xl for section titles, text-base to text-lg for body.\n*   **Contrast discipline**: because backdrops are colorful and variable, headline text often needs a very subtle `text-shadow: 0 1px 12px rgba(0,0,0,0.25)` (dark backdrop) or a light one (light backdrop) purely to keep it legible over the gradient mesh — not a stylistic flourish, a legibility necessity.\n\n### Radius & Shapes\n*   **Standard Radius**: Generously rounded but not pill-shaped by default — `rounded-2xl` (16px) to `rounded-3xl` (24px) for panels/cards; `rounded-xl` (12px) for buttons/inputs; `rounded-full` for avatars, small icon chips, and pill badges.\n*   Soft, consistent radii reinforce the \"pane of glass\" read — never sharp corners (which would break the soft-light metaphor) and never fully organic blobs (that belongs to Organic/Natural, a different system).\n\n### The Glass Effect — THE SIGNATURE MECHANIC\nEvery glass surface is built from four layered ingredients, always together:\n```css\n.glass-panel {\n  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);      /* 1. translucent fill */\n  backdrop-filter: blur(20px) saturate(180%);  /* 2. blur + saturation boost */\n  -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(20px) saturate(180%);\n  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.30); /* 3. defining edge */\n  box-shadow:\n    0 8px 32px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.25),             /* 4a. soft ambient shadow (grounds the panel) */\n    inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.4);     /* 4b. inner top-edge sheen (specular highlight) */\n}\n```\n*   **`backdrop-filter: blur()` is non-negotiable.** A \"glass\" panel with no blur is just a semi-transparent box — it reads as a bug, not a material. Blur radius scales with elevation: 12-16px for small chips, 20-24px for cards, 32-40px for full-screen overlays/modals.\n*   **`saturate(150-200%)`** on the backdrop filter is what makes colors glow richly through the glass instead of looking washed-out and gray — do not omit it.\n*   **The inner top-edge sheen** (`inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,0.4)`) simulates light catching the top lip of a physical glass pane and is what separates \"convincing glass\" from \"blurry rectangle.\"\n*   **Fallback**: where `backdrop-filter` isn't supported (rare, but check target browsers), fall back to a flatter `rgba(255,255,255,0.85)` solid panel — never leave a `backdrop-filter`-only design with no fallback, or unsupported browsers get a broken, nearly-invisible panel.\n\n## Component Stylings\n\n### Buttons\n*   **Primary**: `background: rgba(129, 140, 248, 0.25)` (primary-tinted glass) with the full glass-panel treatment, `border: 1px solid rgba(129,140,248,0.5)`, white text. On hover, opacity/saturation increases (`rgba(129,140,248,0.35)`) and the panel lifts (`-translate-y-0.5`).\n*   **Secondary/Ghost**: Neutral `glass-surface` fill, `glass-border` edge, foreground text.\n*   **Solid CTA (optional escape hatch)**: For the single highest-priority action on a page, a fully opaque `primary` button (no blur) is acceptable — it should stand out precisely *because* everything else is glass.\n*   **Shape**: `rounded-xl` to `rounded-full` depending on size; generous padding (px-6 to px-8).\n*   **Interaction**: `hover:-translate-y-0.5` with an intensified glow (`box-shadow` adds a soft `primary`-colored halo), `active:scale-[0.98]`. Transitions `duration-200 ease-out`.\n\n### Cards / Panels\n*   **Base**: Full glass-panel treatment (fill + blur + border + shadow + sheen) as defined above, `rounded-2xl` to `rounded-3xl`, generous padding (`p-6` to `p-8`).\n*   **Layering**: Glass panels can stack — a glass card on top of the gradient backdrop, with a smaller glass chip on top of *that* card. Each additional layer should increase in opacity slightly (10% → 15% → 20%) so nested glass doesn't disappear into its parent.\n*   **Hover**: `hover:-translate-y-1`, `glass-surface` fill deepens to `glass-surface-strong`, border brightens.\n*   **Never place glass directly on a flat, single-color background** — always ensure there's a gradient mesh or photographic backdrop with enough color variation for the blur to pick up. If the underlying product has a plain white/gray background, add a subtle gradient-mesh layer behind the glass UI specifically to support the effect.\n\n### Inputs\n*   **Style**: `glass-surface` fill (slightly more opaque than cards, ~14%, for legibility of typed text), `glass-border`, `rounded-xl`.\n*   **Focus**: Border brightens to `primary` at full opacity plus a soft `ring-2 ring-primary/30`; background opacity increases slightly so text stays crisp while focused.\n*   **Placeholder**: `foreground-muted`.\n\n### Navigation\n*   **Style**: A glass bar — either a top `sticky` bar spanning full width or a floating `rounded-full`/`rounded-3xl` pill inset from the viewport edges — always with the full glass treatment (this is one of the most recognizable glassmorphism UI patterns, popularized by macOS menu bars and iOS Control Center).\n*   **Scroll behavior**: Blur intensity and fill opacity can *increase* slightly as the user scrolls past the hero, reinforcing that the nav is \"settling\" onto the content beneath it.\n\n### Modals / Overlays\n*   **Backdrop**: darken and blur the page content behind the modal (`backdrop-blur-sm` + `bg-black/30`) so the modal's own glass panel has something rich to refract.\n*   **Panel**: heavier blur (32-40px) and slightly higher fill opacity (~20%) than inline cards, since modals need to stay legible over arbitrary page content.\n\n## Layout & Spacing\n*   **Container Widths**: `max-w-7xl` for marketing/dashboard shells, `max-w-2xl` to `max-w-3xl` for reading-focused content.\n*   **Section Padding**: `py-20` to `py-32`.\n*   **Grid Patterns**: Glass cards read best with generous, even gaps (`gap-6` to `gap-10`) so each panel's blur and border stay visually distinct from its neighbors — overlapping blur regions muddy the effect.\n*   **The Backdrop Layer**: Always implemented as a `fixed`/`absolute` full-bleed layer *behind* all content (`-z-10`), independent of scroll, so the gradient mesh stays a stable, ambient presence rather than scrolling away.\n\n## The \"Bold Factor\" (Signature Elements)\n*   **Visible Blur, Always**: Every panel must show a genuine, visible blur of whatever is behind it — if you can't tell something is blurred behind a \"glass\" element, the effect has failed.\n*   **A Living Gradient Backdrop**: 3-4 large, colorful, slowly-drifting blurred gradient blobs are mandatory background furniture — glass has nothing to refract without them.\n*   **Layered Translucency**: Nest glass-on-glass (a chip inside a card inside a page) with opacity increasing per layer, to build real depth.\n*   **The Top-Edge Sheen**: The `inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,0.4)` highlight on every panel — small but essential; its absence is the #1 reason \"glass\" UIs look like plain translucent boxes.\n*   **Saturation Boost**: `saturate(150-200%)` in the backdrop-filter, always paired with blur.\n\n## Effects & Animation\n*   **Transition Philosophy**: Smooth and airy — `transition-all duration-300 ease-out` for hover/focus states.\n*   **Backdrop Motion**: Gradient blobs drift slowly (10-16s float animations, translate + slight scale), never abruptly — they are ambient lighting, not an attention-grabbing element.\n*   **Hover**: Panels lift (`-translate-y-0.5` to `-translate-y-1`) and their fill/border brighten slightly; never a hard scale-jump.\n*   **Entrance**: Panels fade and blur in (`opacity 0→1`, `blur(8px)→blur(0)` is not practical on the panel itself, so combine `opacity` with a small `translate-y` and `scale-95→100`) — echoing condensation clearing from glass.\n\n## Icons\n*   **Style**: Simple line icons (2px stroke), often housed inside their own small glass chip (`rounded-xl`, `glass-surface` fill) rather than floating bare, so even icon containers participate in the material language.\n*   **Color**: `foreground` or `primary`, with a soft drop-shadow only when placed directly over a busy gradient area (rare, for legibility).\n\n## Accessibility\n*   **Contrast is the primary risk of this style** — translucent panels over a moving, colorful gradient can easily fail contrast checks. Always verify actual rendered contrast (not just the token's nominal value) since the effective background color is `glass-surface` composited over whatever gradient is currently behind it.\n*   **Mitigations**: keep body text opacity ≥ 90% (`foreground`, not `foreground-muted`, for anything below 14px), increase glass fill opacity under text-heavy panels, and avoid placing small text directly over high-contrast points in the gradient mesh.\n*   **Focus States**: A visible `ring-2 ring-primary` plus a border-brighten — never rely on blur/opacity shifts alone as a focus indicator, they're too subtle for many users to perceive.\n*   **Reduced Motion**: Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` — freeze the drifting gradient blobs and disable hover-lift translations, keeping the static blur/translucency (which carries no motion-sickness risk) intact.\n*   **Performance**: `backdrop-filter` is GPU-intensive; on lower-end devices or when many glass panels stack, consider capping the number of simultaneously-blurred layers or reducing blur radius rather than removing the effect outright.\n\n## Responsive Strategy\n*   **Mobile-First**: Reduce blur radius slightly on mobile (e.g. 20px → 14px) for performance; keep the translucency and border treatment intact.\n*   **Breakpoints**: `sm:`/`md:` increase padding and blur intensity; `lg:` unlocks the largest floating-pill navigation and multi-panel bento layouts.\n*   **Navigation**: Floating glass pill collapses to a compact glass bar with a hamburger toggle on mobile; the toggle's dropdown panel is itself a glass surface.\n*   **Backdrop**: Gradient blobs simplify (fewer blobs, less blur) on mobile to protect frame rate, but never disappear entirely — the material metaphor depends on them at every size.\n\n## Applying This System to Any Product\nThis aesthetic is a skin, not a category restriction — apply it the same way regardless of domain:\n*   **Marketing / landing sites**: full gradient-mesh hero with layered glass cards, glass navigation pill, glass CTA panels.\n*   **SaaS dashboards / internal tools**: use glass panels for chrome, filter bars, and modals; for dense data tables, keep row backgrounds a flat, higher-opacity glass (or fully opaque) so text stays crisp — reserve the most dramatic blur/translucency for containers and controls, not every table row.\n*   **Mobile apps**: this style maps directly onto native iOS/Android blur materials (`UIVisualEffectView`, Android's `RenderEffect` blur) — use the platform's native blur API rather than approximating it, and keep the gradient-mesh backdrop as a full-bleed layer behind all screens.\n*   **E-commerce / portfolios**: glass product cards over lifestyle photography (the photo itself becomes the \"backdrop\" the glass refracts), glass filter/sort bars.\n*   **Developer tools / docs**: glass panels for navigation and call-outs; code blocks should stay opaque and high-contrast (never blurred) since code legibility must never be compromised for the aesthetic.\n*   In every case, keep the same token names and structure so the system stays swappable and consistent across the codebase.",
  "examples": [
    "/skill glassmorphism-ui-light   # load the skill, then describe the codebase/product to integrate the design system into",
